# vmm test entry points

`test/vmm/offline_check.sh` is the default non-destructive gate for the
loader and memory ABI.  It compiles userland helpers and uses ordinary files as
fd3/fd4 stand-ins.  It never loads `vmm.ko`, mounts vmmfs, or enters a guest.

The offline gate covers:

- `manifest/manifest_parser_check.sh`: kernel x86 manifest parser boundaries.
- `mem/mem_config_check.sh`: kernel mem config and backing boundaries,
  including two-megabyte alignment, maximum size, formatting, publish/detach,
  default-pager backing shape, machine `vmspace` setup, backing object identity,
  snapshot reference lifetime, max-size lazy prepare without VM faults, GPA
  fault dispatch through the machine `vmspace`, and machine pmap CPU cleanup
  before `vmspace` release.
- `revoke/loader_revoke_compile_check.sh`: fd3/fd4 revoke probe build health
  and ordinary-file no-revoke output-field smoke.
- `smoke/smoke_loader_check.sh`: smoke loader fd3/fd4 output for every mode,
  including sparse fd3 writes, one-page fd4 parsing by the kernel manifest
  parser, and a last-byte sentinel that catches accidental whole-RAM sweeps.
- `nuttx/rtos_nuttx_loader_check.sh`: NuttX loader fd3/fd4 output, sparse fd3
  writes, one-page fd4, kernel manifest parser acceptance, and NuttX boot-data
  layout.  It requires `NUTTX_ELF` or `/var/tmp/nuttx.elf`.
- `linux/linux_kexec_loader_check.sh`: Linux x86_64 kexec-style loader fd3/fd4
  output and manifest parser acceptance using a synthetic bzImage header.  If
  `LINUX_KERNEL` or `/var/tmp/bzImage` is present, it also runs a real-image
  case with optional `LINUX_INITRAMFS`.
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_dma_provider_check.sh`: P7 user-provider compiler gate for
  the `START -> REGISTER -> REGISTERED` exchange and later `STOPPED`/fault
  path.
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_ecam_provider_check.sh`: P3 user-provider compiler gate for
  `START`-driven Linux ECAM enumeration.
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_bar_provider_check.sh`: P4 user-provider compiler gate for
  `START`-driven direct BAR mapping.
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_msix_provider_check.sh`: P5 user-provider compiler gate for
  `START`-driven direct doorbell and MSI-X delivery.
- `linux/linux_initrd_rootfs_build.sh`: builds `/var/tmp/dfvmm-linux-initrd-rootfs.gz`
  from an Alpine initramfs base.  This is the in-memory Linux rootfs used before
  vPCIe exists; it does not require or probe any boot media.

The offline gate proves loader-facing ABI shape and pure kernel-object backing
contracts: fd3/fd4 sizing, sparse-file writes, manifest layout, parser
acceptance, guest RAM object identity, and the expected machine-`vmspace` fault
call shape.  It also proves that `vmm_mem_prepare(VMM_MEM_MAX)` stays at the
object/mapping layer and does not regress into an eager per-page setup loop.  It
does not prove kernel revoke, real pmap/NPT population, TLB shootdown behavior,
or module-unload safety because those require the real kernel pager, vmmfs, and
SVM paths.

Run it from the repository root:

```sh
test/vmm/offline_check.sh
```

`events/events_log_vkernel_test.sh` is a vkernel-only control-plane regression
for the per-machine textual event log.  It does not enter real vCPU execution:
it checks retained reads plus loader-exit and manifest-reject failure messages.
Run it through `~/vkrun2.sh` with a vkernel64 `vmm.ko`.

The following scripts are pc64 true-hardware harnesses.  They load `vmm.ko`,
mount vmmfs, and may enter real guests; run them only as explicit controlled
validation steps with a current pc64 build.  They require `vmm` to be unloaded
on entry and `VMM_KO` to be an absolute path so each run proves the exact module
image selected by the harness:

- `smoke/smoke_exec_test.sh`
- `avic/avic_interrupt_test.sh`
- `revoke/loader_revoke_test.sh`
- `revoke/loader_unload_busy_test.sh`
- `lifecycle/command_atomicity_test.sh`
- `lifecycle/lifecycle_capability_soak_test.sh`
- `lifecycle/stop_delete_test.sh`
- `nuttx/rtos_nuttx_test.sh`
- `linux/linux_initrd_rootfs_test.sh`
- `linux/linux_core_lifecycle_test.sh`
- `linux/linux_console_terminal_test.sh`
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_ecam_test.sh`
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_bar_test.sh`
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_msix_test.sh`
- `pcie/vmm_pcie_dma_test.sh`

`pcie/vmm_pcie_ecam_test.sh` boots the initramfs Linux guest with a provider
that must remain `pending` before machine start.  The provider accepts `START`,
performs generation-matched registration, verifies the BAR and DMA capability
handoff, then the guest console confirms MCFG enumeration of
`0000:00:01.0 [1b36:df01]`.

`pcie/vmm_pcie_bar_test.sh` uses the same pending-to-registered lifecycle,
then verifies that Linux enables `PCI_COMMAND.MEMORY`, reads and writes the
shared BAR page, and that the provider observes the guest write without an
MMIO forwarding loop.

`pcie/vmm_pcie_msix_test.sh` keeps its provider pending until machine start,
requires a generation-matched `REGISTERED` BAR and DMA handoff, then uses the
direct BAR doorbell for completion before the provider sends MSI-X.  Linux
must receive the AVIC-routed interrupt and complete the test driver probe.

`pcie/vmm_pcie_dma_test.sh` uses the boot kernel's existing
`OBJT_MGTDEVICE` and `vm_object_page_remove()` behavior; it needs no paired
VM-core change.  It starts a controlled looping guest, maps the provider's
per-run BAR and DMA capabilities, force-stops the machine, and requires both
retained mappings to fault with `SIGBUS` or `SIGSEGV`.  It then deletes the
machine and unmounts vmmfs while the provider still retains those mappings:
`kldunload vmm` must return `EBUSY`.  After the provider exits, module unload
must succeed.

`lifecycle/lifecycle_capability_soak_test.sh` is the production lifecycle/pager
gate.  It repeats a real one-vCPU start/force-stop/delete/unmount/unload path,
checks fd3/fd4 revoke after loader exit and VM stop, and every tenth round
requires a surviving inherited loader mapping to veto `kldunload` until its
holder exits.  It defaults to 60 rounds; use `VMM_SOAK_ROUNDS=2` only for a
short bring-up check.  Run it after the Linux conformance gate, with a freshly
built pc64 module and no pre-existing `vmm.ko`.

`linux/linux_console_interactive.sh` is the manual terminal harness.  It boots
the in-memory Linux guest and attaches a small stdio terminal bridge to
`machines/<name>/console`; press Ctrl-] to exit, or set `VMM_CONNECTOR=cu` to
try `cu`.
